  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "374bfa0f-12a8-4649-9813-bcede6884937",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Pre-Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "374bfa0f-12a8-4649-9813-bcede6884937",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca98b2de-e516-46ce-91f9-9d5f1872c2e9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cd989f04-6720-44b9-a645-edb7bd71c08f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students reason about the dynamic nature of apps and identify the need for external data sources to keep information current.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Look at this weather app. What information would likely change if you looked at this page tomorrow?*\n- *How does this page get that updated information?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that apps don't store future data internally; they must fetch it from a live source to stay accurate.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cd989f04-6720-44b9-a645-edb7bd71c08f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c3aa1254-9019-45fe-bce7-95381a872457"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce25c4ff-4e33-4bf1-ad4d-ac29b1283993",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Understand that websites communicate with servers via API calls that return structured JSON data, and learn to navigate documentation with AI support.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Decoding the Web"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ce25c4ff-4e33-4bf1-ad4d-ac29b1283993",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "95dec426-0b0d-4f0b-be39-bd1ddc6a61f7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Modern apps are dynamic. Your weather app doesn't store forecasts for every city in the world on your phone. Instead, it fetches that data from a server. Today, we're going to see how that happens.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "95dec426-0b0d-4f0b-be39-bd1ddc6a61f7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c82a7814-03ba-4b58-8ed3-566f3a9e8a5a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide and explain the concept of external data and how apps fetch information from servers to stay up-to-date.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *API*.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use the restaurant analogy: The API is the waiter who takes your request to the kitchen (server) and brings the food (data) back to you (client)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c82a7814-03ba-4b58-8ed3-566f3a9e8a5a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0752aa73-9e7f-4218-b310-14ec5650c314",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0752aa73-9e7f-4218-b310-14ec5650c314",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d22e363-59b7-4f14-b9a5-573447bb2a18",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This interaction is called the Request/Response model. The client asks, and the server provides.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d22e363-59b7-4f14-b9a5-573447bb2a18",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "56c6d32c-ac96-4e97-b8f4-eee3a091a88d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the relationship between the client and the server in the context of an API call.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *JSON*.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that JSON is the 'language' APIs use to send data because it is structured and efficient for computers to process."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "56c6d32c-ac96-4e97-b8f4-eee3a091a88d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16403473-18c8-4fa2-8ab6-7e7ad107da56",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16403473-18c8-4fa2-8ab6-7e7ad107da56",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0869634e-b080-47e8-bb27-5a3581b0ee3d",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain how JSON organizes data using keys and values. Show a simple example on the board if necessary.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Think of a key like a folder label and the value as the document inside that folder."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0869634e-b080-47e8-bb27-5a3581b0ee3d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a905908-5f40-4e86-953c-f8c9c0c6669b",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 4.\n- Level 1: Students read the formal definition of API.\n- Level 2: Students write a personal definition for API and provide one example.\n- Level 3: Students read the formal definition of JSON.\n- Level 4: Students write a personal definition for JSON and provide one example.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who struggle to articulate the concept. Ask: 'If you were building a music app, what other service might you need to talk to?'\n",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: API"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a905908-5f40-4e86-953c-f8c9c0c6669b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2b4735b2-52bd-4aed-a1c7-7cf9ad3ce590",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: JSON",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Check Level 2 and 4 responses. Correct understanding includes the idea of software communication and data sharing."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2b4735b2-52bd-4aed-a1c7-7cf9ad3ce590",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "84f98934-f72c-4fac-8abf-f85a84056b64",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "84f98934-f72c-4fac-8abf-f85a84056b64",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9bbb5d2c-cc3b-4f5a-9536-127117b2a51d",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Demonstrate opening the Network tab and viewing live API requests."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9bbb5d2c-cc3b-4f5a-9536-127117b2a51d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a2686b1a-1be4-490e-a00e-4ccd415ddaba",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We're going to peek behind the curtain of a real website to see exactly what it's asking for and what it gets back.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a2686b1a-1be4-490e-a00e-4ccd415ddaba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "08e09f7f-399f-4473-8408-493000cb0182",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Hand out the API Scavenger Hunt activity guide and the curated PDF documentation for the OpenWeather API.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 5 to complete Levels 5 through 7.\n- Level 5: Students view panel levels to learn about using the Networks Tab.\n- Level 6: Students use the Network Tab in Web Lab to find JSON data.\n- Level 7: Students upload API documentation to AI and ask questions about parameters and data types.\n",
        "progression_name": "Exploring the Network Panel"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "08e09f7f-399f-4473-8408-493000cb0182",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9a2ac1f2-42bd-477e-b421-26af86cddbcf",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Navigate a Weather App"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9a2ac1f2-42bd-477e-b421-26af86cddbcf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "08c88b33-0ba5-426b-bb8d-872c47bbf9a4",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Explore API Documentation",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students get stuck in the Network Tab, remind them to look for the 'Response' or 'Preview' sub-tabs to see the JSON clearly."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "08c88b33-0ba5-426b-bb8d-872c47bbf9a4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "220e837f-f97b-435a-94d3-1cd8ee2b99b7",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "220e837f-f97b-435a-94d3-1cd8ee2b99b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4878f05f-5e06-4ea2-9d5c-45bbd50cc82e",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What happens if an API stops working? How does this affect the user experience?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize system dependencies. If an API fails, the app might lose its primary function (e.g., a weather app with no weather).\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4878f05f-5e06-4ea2-9d5c-45bbd50cc82e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8266aa4d-ac8f-40ac-971a-91de8ecf19b1",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Using APIs makes our apps powerful, but it also means we depend on other services to keep working.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8266aa4d-ac8f-40ac-971a-91de8ecf19b1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "67fffe5d-432f-464b-b47c-871b7832897a",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Use the PRIM (Predict, Run, Investigate, Modify) framework to interact with live code and understand how modifying request attributes changes the data displayed on screen.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "My First API Call"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "67fffe5d-432f-464b-b47c-871b7832897a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82179a01-f062-45cd-b46d-9ab499cab609",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that we've seen how APIs work behind the scenes, it's time for you to make your first API call! We'll use a framework called PRIM to help us understand the code step-by-step.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82179a01-f062-45cd-b46d-9ab499cab609",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1dee36c-c604-4e3d-9e0c-499c99a50597",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the PRIM framework to the students."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1dee36c-c604-4e3d-9e0c-499c99a50597",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "25914a87-2fb1-4770-93ec-b6a05313a523",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In JavaScript, we use a special command called fetch to talk to APIs. Think of it like sending a digital messenger to a server with a specific address, called an endpoint. The server then sends a response back with the data we requested.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "25914a87-2fb1-4770-93ec-b6a05313a523",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cbeb7702-dd25-4dc3-b753-9e469c629bf1",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 7 to complete Levels 8 through 9.\n- Level 8: Students choose an API and predict its output based on the code snippet.\n- Level 9: Students run the code, investigate with AI, and modify attributes to see new data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who are unsure which API to pick. Suggest PokeAPI for a structured experience or NASA for visual data. Watch for students who forget to click 'Run' after modifying code. Ask: 'What did you change in the request, and how did the response look different?'\n",
        "progression_name": "Make a Prediction",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Compare a fetch call to ordering food. The menu is the documentation, the order is the request, and the meal is the response data."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cbeb7702-dd25-4dc3-b753-9e469c629bf1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "72d92016-3310-4205-9e40-e31f42831d5d",
      "position": 25,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Modify an API Call",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Check that students are using the AI Tutor on Level 8 to explain parts of the fetch call. Correct understanding involves identifying the URL and the data being requested."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "72d92016-3310-4205-9e40-e31f42831d5d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "70eb2883-f790-44cf-ba73-6b5e73c4e432",
      "position": 26,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "70eb2883-f790-44cf-ba73-6b5e73c4e432",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f0c0579c-dc13-4df9-9b77-8229773e31cd",
      "position": 27,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What happened when you modified the code? How did the API response change?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that changing the parameters in the request (the URL or attributes) directly changes what the server sends back. Listen for: 'I changed the ID and got a different character' or 'I changed the date and the image updated.'\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f0c0579c-dc13-4df9-9b77-8229773e31cd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59a177b-a6bc-4169-874a-ecf26277e0ae"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f11d0408-ee82-4803-884f-e15c2a0a3d1b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f11d0408-ee82-4803-884f-e15c2a0a3d1b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "65d6b948-1f58-4580-8a1c-f44ec983f913"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "afbeba77-3344-43a2-b6db-3043e1a0f9da",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 10 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "afbeba77-3344-43a2-b6db-3043e1a0f9da",
        "lesson_activity.key": "65d6b948-1f58-4580-8a1c-f44ec983f913"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93f09173-bbf8-437a-8fb8-e8378fd7a878",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Build \"system awareness\" by identifying visual cues of loading and differentiating between static (code) and dynamic (data) content."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93f09173-bbf8-437a-8fb8-e8378fd7a878",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d8a8a304-f7ea-49ed-b871-4cdf61b64c8d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef596f40-da44-451b-bd4c-7f571b8905e6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Have you ever opened an app like Spotify or Instagram while in Airplane Mode? You might see the buttons and the logo, but the content is missing. Today we are exploring why that happens and how code handles the \"wait\" for that data.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef596f40-da44-451b-bd4c-7f571b8905e6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d8a8a304-f7ea-49ed-b871-4cdf61b64c8d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "26b4d914-acfc-4945-a046-85aa6d92ac75",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Imagine opening an app offline. What changes? What stays the same?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify that the app layout (buttons, icons) is there, but live data (songs, weather) is missing or replaced by loading icons.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the difference between content built into the app's code (static) and content fetched from a server (dynamic).\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use a physical analogy: The \"Static Content\" is like the menu at a restaurant (it's printed and ready), while \"Dynamic Content\" is the food (it has to be made and brought to you after you order)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "26b4d914-acfc-4945-a046-85aa6d92ac75",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d8a8a304-f7ea-49ed-b871-4cdf61b64c8d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3e488a2e-f3df-4b95-a6bc-7aec334a8bbc",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3e488a2e-f3df-4b95-a6bc-7aec334a8bbc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d8a8a304-f7ea-49ed-b871-4cdf61b64c8d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a209302d-07f7-4838-b6bc-7f10cf754584",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How does an app tell you it is \"thinking\" or \"waiting\"?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify visual cues like spinners, progress bars, skeleton screens, or gray boxes. This reinforces that the app is aware it is waiting for data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a209302d-07f7-4838-b6bc-7f10cf754584",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d8a8a304-f7ea-49ed-b871-4cdf61b64c8d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1c3f4479-1944-442d-93be-c942136fc78e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Model the difference between synchronous and asynchronous execution using a real-world parallel.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "The Restaurant Analogy"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1c3f4479-1944-442d-93be-c942136fc78e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ade1968-278a-4105-9703-cd7453fc13fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5fdcb0be-cd9a-4361-9609-6bc930a01fb4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine you are at a restaurant where the chef only does one thing at a time. They put water on to boil, then they stand there and watch it until it's done. Only then do they start chopping vegetables.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5fdcb0be-cd9a-4361-9609-6bc930a01fb4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ade1968-278a-4105-9703-cd7453fc13fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "feae2244-6eeb-4b5d-90f6-8014d6f8deec",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How efficient is a kitchen where the chef boils water, waits, then chops veggies?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students realize that waiting for every single task to finish before starting the next is inefficient and creates a bad experience for the customer.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What can a chef do while they are waiting for the water to boil to prepare the meal more efficiently?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify that multitasking—prepping sauce or chopping while water heats—allows the meal to be finished much faster.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *synchronous code*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *asynchronous code*.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "feae2244-6eeb-4b5d-90f6-8014d6f8deec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ade1968-278a-4105-9703-cd7453fc13fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ea1c0f93-ca3b-4dfa-ab5c-5116a7c1a7cf",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Demonstrate the pain point of synchronous (blocking) programming in a user interface.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "The Frozen App"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ea1c0f93-ca3b-4dfa-ab5c-5116a7c1a7cf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ade1968-278a-4105-9703-cd7453fc13fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6086316-01ff-4d8c-826f-f58dfd0975a3",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that we’ve defined these terms, it’s time to see them in action. You’re going to head into 'The Frozen App' to run an experiment: watch what happens to to the display as a user when you trigger a heavy synchronous task. Does the app stay 'alive,' or does it completely ignore you?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6086316-01ff-4d8c-826f-f58dfd0975a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ade1968-278a-4105-9703-cd7453fc13fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd475ae8-0b64-4f8d-bc75-1b58ef13338b",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 5.\n- Level 1: Students read the definition and example of synchronous programming.\n- Level 2: Students write their own definition of synchronous programming and include a real-world example.\n- Level 3: Students read the definition and example of asynchronous programming.\n- Level 4: Students write their own definition of asynchronous programming and include a real-world example.\n- Level 5: Students run a demo of an application that has a long synchronous operation and observe how it impact the user experience.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** For Level 1 and 2, look for students who are confusing \"synchronous\" with \"simultaneous.\" Remind them that \"sync\" in programming means things happen one after another, waiting for the previous step to finish. For Level 3 and 4, listen for students explaining that the program \"keeps going\" while something loads. Ask: \"In an app, what is an example of something that might take time to load?\"\n",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Synchronous Programming"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd475ae8-0b64-4f8d-bc75-1b58ef13338b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ade1968-278a-4105-9703-cd7453fc13fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fa178839-b64d-4bb6-93ea-9e05c5799077",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Asynchronous Programming"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fa178839-b64d-4bb6-93ea-9e05c5799077",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ade1968-278a-4105-9703-cd7453fc13fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "81b9c484-531f-4240-b04b-7f0b836fa060",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Demo: Run the Frozen App"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "81b9c484-531f-4240-b04b-7f0b836fa060",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ade1968-278a-4105-9703-cd7453fc13fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f2eb98da-ba14-4583-8ebc-4af6b8397867",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which specific action was a synchronous operation? Which specific action was an asynchronous operation? How did you know the difference just by looking at the screen?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Listen for students connecting the technical behavior to the user impact. For the like button (asynchronous), students should note that they could keep interact with other parts of the app immediately. For the compress video button (synchronous), students should explicitly mention the 5-second freeze. Listen for phrases like \"The app stopped responding,\" or \"I thought the app crashed because I couldn't even hit the 'Like' button.\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If an app freezes every time you click a button, how does that change your trust in the app? Would you assume it’s working, or would you think it crashed?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students to articulate that a synchronous (blocking) task doesn't just slow down the app; it makes the user feel like the app is broken or unresponsive. Asynchronous programming is a fundamental user experience tool used to keep the user feeling in control and informed.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Why Apps Wait activity guide.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f2eb98da-ba14-4583-8ebc-4af6b8397867",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ade1968-278a-4105-9703-cd7453fc13fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b110bf84-7919-47ed-9945-84474712a813",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students learn how asynchronous code works by predicting and investigating execution order in Web Lab.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 6 to complete Levels 6 through 8.\n- Level 6: Students predict how blocking code freezes the interface while tasks complete one at a time.\n- Level 7: Students predict the execution order of multiple kitchen tasks running on different timers.\n- Level 8: Students predict the outcome when the chef tries to serve food before the cooking timer finishes.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Analogy: Synchronous vs. Asynchronous",
        "progression_name": "Analogy: Synchronous vs. Asynchronous ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Have students use AI Chat to explain setTimeout() role in the code. In JavaScript, we use a function called setTimeout to simulate that \"cooking time\" or a network delay. Explain that setTimeout takes a function and a delay in milliseconds. Crucially, emphasize that it is asynchronous—the rest of the program keeps running while the timer counts down."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 8 is a critical \"Aha!\" moment. Ensure students understand that trying to use data before it has \"cooked\" results in an error."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b110bf84-7919-47ed-9945-84474712a813",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ade1968-278a-4105-9703-cd7453fc13fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df9221c6-f213-409c-a88e-22125345f817",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df9221c6-f213-409c-a88e-22125345f817",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ade1968-278a-4105-9703-cd7453fc13fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f668f707-f119-4930-8923-9fdcd51e2a8f",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Why did the \"Serving customer\" line print \"undefined\" even though the burger was cooking?*\n- *How can we make sure our app doesn't try to use data before it arrives?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students synthesize their learning from the coding exploration. They should identify that since the kitchen (setTimeout) was working in the background, the main program moved on to the next line immediately. Because the data wasn't \"ready\" yet, the variable was still empty (undefined).\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f668f707-f119-4930-8923-9fdcd51e2a8f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ade1968-278a-4105-9703-cd7453fc13fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d4076794-0dc9-44a6-ac2a-e826d39b080f",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Visually map the flow of time in asynchronous operations using the code students just investigated.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Tracing the Timeline"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d4076794-0dc9-44a6-ac2a-e826d39b080f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ade1968-278a-4105-9703-cd7453fc13fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eccb2b06-97fe-437f-8b00-27690c2930d9",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let's map out exactly what happened in each kitchen using a timeline graph.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
