    {
      "key": "a72089ee-fb32-476f-9077-6507c8f431ac",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 7 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a72089ee-fb32-476f-9077-6507c8f431ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e85c10e1-6dd3-45a0-a844-0d228de41526"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "42803c4d-54ba-4b6d-a916-26766b0ea6f4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students use a real-world analogy to understand the states of an asynchronous request.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** You ordered a product and check its delivery status. What would each of these mean for your package? (Pending, Fulfilled, Rejected)\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students visualize the waiting period and the possible outcomes of a delivery. This sets the stage for the concept of a Promise.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle, give them hints: 'Pending' is the time the mail truck is driving. 'Fulfilled' is a happy arrival. 'Rejected' is a 'Package Lost' notification."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "42803c4d-54ba-4b6d-a916-26766b0ea6f4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "01fed83d-7f89-4daa-8476-941440fb85dd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "db9141ec-c92a-48c8-9a07-0a5804ca0840",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "db9141ec-c92a-48c8-9a07-0a5804ca0840",
        "lesson_activity.key": "01fed83d-7f89-4daa-8476-941440fb85dd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f76c6e88-4e7e-4fa5-92dc-106d3722445e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explicitly connect the student definitions to the JavaScript Promise language: Pending, Fulfilled, and Rejected."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f76c6e88-4e7e-4fa5-92dc-106d3722445e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "01fed83d-7f89-4daa-8476-941440fb85dd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bc3d63f7-d6d1-40a9-9905-961bbc82c17a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In JavaScript, when we fetch data, it doesn’t happen instantly. Instead, JavaScript gives us something called a Promise. A Promise starts in the Pending state — that means the result is not ready yet. It’s still “in transit.” Eventually, the Promise resolves in one of two ways: It becomes Fulfilled if the data arrives successfully, or Rejected if something goes wrong. Just like your package, it can’t be both — it ends in exactly one final state.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bc3d63f7-d6d1-40a9-9905-961bbc82c17a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "01fed83d-7f89-4daa-8476-941440fb85dd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f919183-21d7-4de3-9a9b-97afd04d34fc",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f919183-21d7-4de3-9a9b-97afd04d34fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "01fed83d-7f89-4daa-8476-941440fb85dd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1162f2c8-fdc5-4dee-afe4-806a3cda02bb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *Promise*.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use the tracking number analogy. You don't have the package yet, but you have a guarantee that it is either coming, or you'll be told if the delivery failed."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1162f2c8-fdc5-4dee-afe4-806a3cda02bb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eed9ef9f-58bf-4235-8283-1715f9c08267"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a1df8b4d-712c-4beb-a0c7-ecbebd8eb4ce",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a1df8b4d-712c-4beb-a0c7-ecbebd8eb4ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eed9ef9f-58bf-4235-8283-1715f9c08267"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8fd4150f-683e-4189-96de-f1bdba0320d7",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the three states of a Promise – Pending, Fulfilled, and Rejected."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8fd4150f-683e-4189-96de-f1bdba0320d7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eed9ef9f-58bf-4235-8283-1715f9c08267"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4c97f17f-772f-400b-8007-02d686e688e9",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Using our earlier package analogy, let’s walk through the stages of a Promise for a fetch() request. When we call fetch(), it sends a request to a server and immediately returns a Promise. That Promise represents the future result of that request.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4c97f17f-772f-400b-8007-02d686e688e9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eed9ef9f-58bf-4235-8283-1715f9c08267"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a6af926-e9de-4fb4-a242-7cea0cabae22",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation to explain each stage.\n- Fetch Request: This is the moment we call fetch(). The request is sent to the server. fetch() does not wait for the answer. It immediately gives us a Promise object.\n- Pending: As soon as the request is sent, the Promise is in the Pending state. Pending means the request is still in progress. We don’t have data yet. We don’t have an error yet. We are waiting.\n- Fulfilled: If the server responds successfully, the Promise becomes Fulfilled. That usually means we received a successful HTTP response, like 200 OK, and we now have data to work with. The Promise has resolved successfully.\n- Rejected: If something goes wrong — maybe the server crashes, the network fails, or the request can’t complete — the Promise becomes Rejected. Instead of data, we receive an error.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If the Promise is Fulfilled and we finally have the data, what do we want our app to do next? If we don’t handle the Rejected state, what might happen in our app?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect Promise outcomes to app behavior – displaying data on success and responding appropriately when something goes wrong. Students realize that we must attach different code paths for success and failure when working with asynchronous data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the then() method.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the catch() method.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 1 to complete Levels 3 through 5.\n- Level 1: Students read the definition and example of Promise.\n- Level 2: Students write their own definition of Promise.\n- Level 3: Students read the definition and example of .then() and .catch().\n- Level 4: Students write their own definition of .then().\n- Level 5: Students write their own definition of .catch().\n",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Promise"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a6af926-e9de-4fb4-a242-7cea0cabae22",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eed9ef9f-58bf-4235-8283-1715f9c08267"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "00221227-7d99-49ef-8a4f-90abbbb1e414",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: .then() and .catch()"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "00221227-7d99-49ef-8a4f-90abbbb1e414",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eed9ef9f-58bf-4235-8283-1715f9c08267"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9984e269-3fb5-446b-b545-8befe426cc91",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you notice and wonder about the number of .then() and .catch() calls in the code below?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students understand that each .then() passes its result to the next step in the chain, while .catch() handles failures. Students recognize that Promise chains can have multiple .then() calls for handling successful steps, and one .catch() to handle errors at the end. Students notice that each .then() passes its result to the next step in the chain, while .catch() handles failures.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 6 to complete Levels 6 through 7.\n- Level 6: Students read 'The Unboxing Rule' panel explaining why we use two .then() calls.\n- Level 7: Students trace a Promise chain and identify the state of a Promise in a code snippet.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9984e269-3fb5-446b-b545-8befe426cc91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eed9ef9f-58bf-4235-8283-1715f9c08267"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c869e0a2-763f-416c-bb8b-1443f81428b5",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "The Unboxing Rule"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c869e0a2-763f-416c-bb8b-1443f81428b5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eed9ef9f-58bf-4235-8283-1715f9c08267"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a8817e7-632f-4b60-9315-7b692507b6cd",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Trace the Promise Chain"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a8817e7-632f-4b60-9315-7b692507b6cd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eed9ef9f-58bf-4235-8283-1715f9c08267"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "97570577-71ac-4706-a08a-fd9b3530d37d",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through the timeline of an asynchronous request."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "97570577-71ac-4706-a08a-fd9b3530d37d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eed9ef9f-58bf-4235-8283-1715f9c08267"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "adbaef29-8856-4cbb-8ab4-57e0b8b4d501",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This timeline shows that code doesn't just run top-to-bottom instantly. There's a 'waiting room' where the Promise sits until the server answers.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that .catch() is like a safety net. If the internet goes down or the URL is wrong, .catch() keeps the app from crashing."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "adbaef29-8856-4cbb-8ab4-57e0b8b4d501",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eed9ef9f-58bf-4235-8283-1715f9c08267"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "78fcc4c3-fc9b-49e9-b1b1-c7da7e5d0b98",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 8 to write their own Promise chain.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** On Levels 8, ensure students are putting their DOM update code inside the second .then(). If they put it outside, it will run before the data exists.\n",
        "progression_name": "Build a Promise Chain",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For Level 8, suggest students temporarily break their URL (e.g., change 'api' to 'apix') to test if their .catch() block actually works."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "78fcc4c3-fc9b-49e9-b1b1-c7da7e5d0b98",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8f887029-dceb-4721-91de-811901cd9749"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4f78b5c5-7ee7-40d1-9616-7f52a9942303",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4f78b5c5-7ee7-40d1-9616-7f52a9942303",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cbff52e4-4119-44ae-9c0d-8003e68f31fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50d5c5ce-4b00-4930-a09f-6355784d1451",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 9 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50d5c5ce-4b00-4930-a09f-6355784d1451",
