      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fefc7d65-b4ac-4478-b939-3bb196716f74",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students connect the concept of \"waiting\" to real-world tasks to understand when await is necessary in code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Think about tasks you complete in your morning routine. What can you do at the same time? What has to wait for something else before it can happen?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify that some tasks are independent (checking phone while getting dressed) while others have dependencies (can't get on the bus until it arrives). This mirrors how code waits for a server response.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fefc7d65-b4ac-4478-b939-3bb196716f74",
        "lesson_activity.key": "653e991f-fada-4eec-9141-c7bb25d80f96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8fbeda3f-e638-4005-af6f-671cafe69428",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Notice that some of your tasks can happen at the same time. They don’t depend on anything else. But other tasks can’t start until something else finishes. You can’t get on the bus until it arrives. You can’t eat toast until it’s done toasting. In programming, we run into the same situation. Some code can run right away. But sometimes, our program has to wait for something, like data from a server, before it can continue.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8fbeda3f-e638-4005-af6f-671cafe69428",
        "lesson_activity.key": "653e991f-fada-4eec-9141-c7bb25d80f96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f4829f60-90b3-4366-98a0-1b8570aa2439",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f4829f60-90b3-4366-98a0-1b8570aa2439",
        "lesson_activity.key": "653e991f-fada-4eec-9141-c7bb25d80f96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7987979e-9823-422b-865f-33d8ee0f164a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Practice identifying \"waiting steps\" and translating a sequence of actions into a structured, asynchronous format.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Hand out the Making Steps Clear Activity Guide for today's lesson.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Morning Routine"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7987979e-9823-422b-865f-33d8ee0f164a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ec102383-f268-4228-9cc9-48c8342e83b7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Think about your morning routine. Some things happen at once, but some things force you to stop and wait. In computer science, we call this asynchronous behavior. When we fetch data from a server, it isn't instant. If our code was 'synchronous,' the whole app would freeze until the data arrived. Asynchronous code lets the background work happen while the app stays active.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ec102383-f268-4228-9cc9-48c8342e83b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8bbbfa6d-4ed8-407e-bb0b-bab44e85beb8",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the difference between synchronous and asynchronous code.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use the analogy of ordering food. Synchronous is standing at the counter until it's ready. Asynchronous is taking a buzzer to your table so you can talk to friends while you wait."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8bbbfa6d-4ed8-407e-bb0b-bab44e85beb8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3c6928c2-d23f-4c26-9517-c232471d5f82",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3c6928c2-d23f-4c26-9517-c232471d5f82",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "02946277-a608-4233-b6f8-d0697054337c",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now it's your turn. Look at your morning routine. Which steps make you stop? Write them out and use the word 'await' for the ones that involve waiting for something else to finish.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "02946277-a608-4233-b6f8-d0697054337c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6ef14b9-c390-442e-b9ed-a70b936082b0",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students write pseudocode for their morning routine in their activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who mark every step with await. Remind them that await is only for things outside their control, like a toaster or a bus. Ask: 'Can you brush your teeth while the toast is cooking?'\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Check if students correctly identify external dependencies versus internal actions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6ef14b9-c390-442e-b9ed-a70b936082b0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb7e10b6-dab9-4db5-a72c-7dc9eaa6df85",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb7e10b6-dab9-4db5-a72c-7dc9eaa6df85",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd7eb766-0ea3-4803-8337-71e89465ab84",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the async keyword.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Think of 'async' as a label on a box. It tells JavaScript, 'Inside this box, we are going to be doing some waiting.'"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd7eb766-0ea3-4803-8337-71e89465ab84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
