        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6bb644f-553e-4b01-a973-c3fda7d5de8d",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the async/await syntax structure.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Contrast this with the 'Promise' syntax from the last lesson. Point out how the variable assignment happens directly on the left side of the await call."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6bb644f-553e-4b01-a973-c3fda7d5de8d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e2429b9-fac8-4fec-8ab2-e6e4f249682b",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e2429b9-fac8-4fec-8ab2-e6e4f249682b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d748b8b7-0810-4e63-a798-5ee01b00fd81",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Understand the readability benefits of async/await by directly comparing it to .then() syntax.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Comparing Promise Chains"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d748b8b7-0810-4e63-a798-5ee01b00fd81",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "06c18a6d-34ec-4903-a7d7-41f935b4359b",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let's look at these side-by-side. Both of these code snippets do the exact same thing, but they look very different.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "06c18a6d-34ec-4903-a7d7-41f935b4359b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b772dfef-9a55-47d2-9cdc-5132b94ca679",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students compare the .then() chains to the async/await syntax in their Activity Guides.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Which version would you rather debug?*\n- *Why does top-to-bottom flow help?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that async/await is easier to read because it follows the standard linear flow of programming, making it easier to track variables and logic.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b772dfef-9a55-47d2-9cdc-5132b94ca679",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09c8189f-ba76-48eb-9232-8158857a0128",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Manually apply refactoring skills and use AI to evaluate and compare results.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 3 to complete Levels 1 through 4.\n- Level 1: Students read the definition and example of async/await.\n- Level 2: Students write their own definition of async/await.\n- Level 3: Students use an AI tutor to write refactored code for their random joke API.\n- Level 4: Students manually refactor the code and decide which version (AI or human) to use and justify their choice.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ask students: 'Did the AI use await correctly? Did it keep the variables named the same way you did?' Encourage them to find one difference between their code and the AI's code.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Human vs AI Refactoring",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: async / await"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09c8189f-ba76-48eb-9232-8158857a0128",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "74277a20-b9d0-41fa-80ee-8818e27d6fc1",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Use AI to Refactor the Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "74277a20-b9d0-41fa-80ee-8818e27d6fc1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5493ae10-e845-4c8f-b241-3a2222f82523",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Manually Refactor the Code",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Verify that the code still functions correctly after the manual refactor."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5493ae10-e845-4c8f-b241-3a2222f82523",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1397cdb8-2720-4b05-a34e-e92799302c4d",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1397cdb8-2720-4b05-a34e-e92799302c4d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d10d357f-b370-4d7f-88c2-ea8bc2d4d8ec",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *When is it better to use AI?*\n- *Why check AI code for errors?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on the 'Human-in-the-Loop' concept, recognizing that while AI speeds up refactoring, humans must ensure the logic and execution order remain correct.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d10d357f-b370-4d7f-88c2-ea8bc2d4d8ec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "263e787c-4def-48dd-bfdf-1f83b7fdac93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f7b49539-72a6-4407-85a9-4b795b8b52a4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f7b49539-72a6-4407-85a9-4b795b8b52a4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9d1a7516-5fde-41a1-818b-59b46354a5d2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a8ba792-82af-45cf-a8de-13afdf93ac31",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 5 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a8ba792-82af-45cf-a8de-13afdf93ac31",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9d1a7516-5fde-41a1-818b-59b46354a5d2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7ba4390c-dfc1-4bfb-aec2-b6647f05db29",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Engage students with the idea that UI elements come from specific pieces of JSON data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show a small UI mockup card (title, subtitle, one stat/label) next to an unexplained raw JSON response. Give students a quiet moment to scan both.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If you were building this app, which 3 pieces of information would you want from this data? What looks unuseful?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students start to see that not all data from an API is equally important. Guide them toward identifying a small set of key fields that directly match what they see on the UI card (for example, name, a description, one key stat) and labeling the rest as “nice to have” or “extra.”\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7ba4390c-dfc1-4bfb-aec2-b6647f05db29",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e2b75db0-d059-495c-bb65-ce1b7fe417fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "855c79d1-c8aa-413a-b27e-32286b527821",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we will learn how to read this \"strange\" JSON text as organized data and turn it into something we can loop over and display in our apps.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "855c79d1-c8aa-413a-b27e-32286b527821",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e2b75db0-d059-495c-bb65-ce1b7fe417fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "06a90452-5194-4d8e-9ae1-f3bca7898b0c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "06a90452-5194-4d8e-9ae1-f3bca7898b0c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e2b75db0-d059-495c-bb65-ce1b7fe417fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "34b52363-eb6e-4da0-a3ab-12d12ccf59a9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students recognize arrays, objects, and primitives as the structural “shape” of JSON.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly explain that JSON has patterns: square brackets for arrays, curly braces for objects, and different kinds of values. Tell students they will annotate a JSON snippet to make these patterns visible.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 1 to work in Sketch Lab. They will annotate a JSON snippet by highlighting arrays [], objects {}, and labeling example values as strings, numbers, or booleans.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students correctly identifying arrays, objects, primitives, and nested structures. Ask questions like: How do you know this is an array? What tells you this value is nested inside something else? Support students who feel overwhelmed by helping them focus on one bracket and character type at a time.\n",
        "progression_name": "Seeing the Shape of the JSON"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "34b52363-eb6e-4da0-a3ab-12d12ccf59a9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f5663704-a466-4c47-97ef-5657dde82e85"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5aa27120-ee58-4c20-845f-7fdd37bcba43",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When you can see where things live inside the JSON, you can follow a path to grab exactly the value you need. The next step is learning the pattern we use to write those paths.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5aa27120-ee58-4c20-845f-7fdd37bcba43",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f5663704-a466-4c47-97ef-5657dde82e85"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1c50acd2-911c-42a0-9ad7-af70c73e2479",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *dot notation*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 4.\n- Level 2: Students read the definition and example of dot notation.\n- Level 3: Students write their own definition of dot notation.\n- Level 4: Use the JSON snippet to write dot-notation paths.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students write valid paths that match the JSON (no guessing field names). Ask: Where do you see that property in the JSON? What comes first in your path? Support students by having them point in the JSON and say the path out loud before writing it.\n",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Dot Notation"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1c50acd2-911c-42a0-9ad7-af70c73e2479",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f5663704-a466-4c47-97ef-5657dde82e85"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c73f24cb-a846-4612-9077-ed4538a80af5",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Trace a Dot-Notation Path",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students keep guessing paths, have them trace with their finger from the top-level object down to the value, saying each key out loud as before turning it into code.."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c73f24cb-a846-4612-9077-ed4538a80af5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f5663704-a466-4c47-97ef-5657dde82e85"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff09c849-f01e-43c4-80ca-f6dc36ff10ac",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define .map().\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Connect dot notation to the new idea of transforming arrays. Explain that .map() lets you walk through an array and build a cleaner version of each item that is easier to use in the UI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 5 to complete Levels 5 through 7.\n- Level 5: Students read the formal definition of .map().\n- Level 6: Students write a personal definition for .map() and provide one example.\n- Level 7: Imagine a info card. Choose three properties, give each a clear key name in the clean object, match each to a dot-notation path in the raw JSON, and explain why it is useful for the UI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who choose UI-relevant fields and pair them with correct dot-notation paths. Prompt with: What will each item in your new array look like? How will this help you loop and render cards later?\n",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: .map()"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff09c849-f01e-43c4-80ca-f6dc36ff10ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f5663704-a466-4c47-97ef-5657dde82e85"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3c47d8ca-04cf-46f4-85c7-4cf31efc8b54",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Design a Mapped Object for the UI",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 7 responses to check if students can connect JSON structure, dot notation, and UI needs. Strong responses pick appropriate fields, write valid dot-notation paths, and give a clear reason each field helps the UI. Offer quick feedback or conferencing with students who only copy raw field names without thinking about the UI."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3c47d8ca-04cf-46f4-85c7-4cf31efc8b54",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f5663704-a466-4c47-97ef-5657dde82e85"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b4be81a6-8898-4ed1-b75a-5016fa7ea825",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b4be81a6-8898-4ed1-b75a-5016fa7ea825",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f5663704-a466-4c47-97ef-5657dde82e85"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3eb93050-05d7-463b-a677-ff918d68aede",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How did understanding the shape of the JSON help you write dot-notation paths? How did dot notation and .map() work together to get UI-ready data?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect the full progression: see JSON structure → write accurate dot-notation paths → use .map() plus those paths to design clean, loopable data for the UI. Listen for students mentioning arrays/objects, specific paths, and the idea of building a new, simpler array.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3eb93050-05d7-463b-a677-ff918d68aede",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f5663704-a466-4c47-97ef-5657dde82e85"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af387ca2-e4e2-4ce8-ab7e-3811a37acd18",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af387ca2-e4e2-4ce8-ab7e-3811a37acd18",
        "lesson_activity.key": "784eb74f-91aa-41b8-bf65-4ef6b1e07f13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "144fa912-e6f4-41ff-8e6f-b9ce5d4b2607",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 8 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "144fa912-e6f4-41ff-8e6f-b9ce5d4b2607",
        "lesson_activity.key": "784eb74f-91aa-41b8-bf65-4ef6b1e07f13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7580e734-4ce0-4ba7-881b-d26b6540123a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students see that the same data can be visualized in multiple ways."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7580e734-4ce0-4ba7-881b-d26b6540123a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "35f5355a-0cfa-4ac1-84de-1718a8ca2aa4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "359d05f2-7e1e-462c-9b6b-344f7cc0def5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Look at these two ways of showing the same dishes. One is cards, the other is a list. Both are representations of the exact same data, but they serve different user needs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "359d05f2-7e1e-462c-9b6b-344f7cc0def5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "35f5355a-0cfa-4ac1-84de-1718a8ca2aa4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b525b459-1ebc-43ed-a667-d9f0f208ebd7",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *When is it more helpful for a user to see the data as a card layout?*\n- *When is it more helpful to see it in a list view?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students realize layout depends on user needs (e.g., searching vs. browsing). Lists are better for sorting and comparing information quickly, while cards are better for visual exploration and featuring images.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b525b459-1ebc-43ed-a667-d9f0f208ebd7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "35f5355a-0cfa-4ac1-84de-1718a8ca2aa4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "47f60254-effc-49c3-abb4-3d89f364e1e8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students learn how apps display live data from API calls and how to parse JSON so it can be used effectively in a program.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *data-driven content*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain what it means to parse JSON.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Understanding Data-Driven Content",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Check if students understand that parsing is the 'translation' step between raw data and usable code. Ask: 'Why can't we just use the raw string directly in our HTML?' Use the analogy of reading a sentence. You 'parse' the words to find the subject and the verb so you can understand the meaning. Computers do the same thing with JSON strings to turn them into JavaScript objects."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "47f60254-effc-49c3-abb4-3d89f364e1e8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6cc520cf-4bc7-4af2-a71d-49b532f76f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c00216a2-cf00-415e-a5fc-bb9099585e2a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 4.\n- Level 1: Students read the formal definition of Data-Driven Content.\n- Level 2: Students write a personal definition for Data-Driven Content and provide one example.\n- Level 3: Students read the formal definition of Parsing JSON.\n- Level 4: Students write a personal definition for Parsing JSON and provide one example.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who can connect the concept to apps they use daily. Ask: “What part of Instagram is data-driven? What part is the same every time the app loads?“ Misconception: Students may think all text on a page is data-driven. Clarify that static titles or buttons are not usually data-driven.\n",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Data-Driven Content"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c00216a2-cf00-415e-a5fc-bb9099585e2a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6cc520cf-4bc7-4af2-a71d-49b532f76f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6339c074-0207-43c7-9db5-d9a43ec1c687",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Parsing JSON"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6339c074-0207-43c7-9db5-d9a43ec1c687",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6cc520cf-4bc7-4af2-a71d-49b532f76f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f84ce953-023b-48a7-b111-0d8fc9d8ade6",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students act as UI designers to decide which data fields from JSON are relevant and how they should be visually arranged.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why is it important to parse the JSON data before designing and coding our web app?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should understand that parsing the JSON data allows the computer to interpret the information properly so it can be displayed on screen. Students should read over the JSON themselves to be able to plan out their design before coding. This prevents errors and ensures the UI displays the most relevant data for the app's purpose. It's much easier to sketch a blueprint than to rewrite complex loop logic later.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 5 to design a visual mockup of a content card or playlist view in Sketch Lab.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Designing the Blueprint",
        "progression_name": "Sketch Your Music App",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students should use AI Chat in Level 5 if they need support brainstorming their app layout. Consider distributing the Showing Data on the Page activity guide for students to plan out their idea on paper."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f84ce953-023b-48a7-b111-0d8fc9d8ade6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6cc520cf-4bc7-4af2-a71d-49b532f76f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d136379f-65b3-4f3d-8f52-3e30a054a0bf",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d136379f-65b3-4f3d-8f52-3e30a054a0bf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6cc520cf-4bc7-4af2-a71d-49b532f76f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6e6ec317-f8b6-461f-89ad-4627afbbe2a8",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students practice rendering data-driven content using loops and learn to fix common bugs caused by inconsistent backend data.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Building from the Blueprint"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6e6ec317-f8b6-461f-89ad-4627afbbe2a8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6cc520cf-4bc7-4af2-a71d-49b532f76f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d632c65c-9e48-49d4-b253-32faa74f8be8",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that we have a blueprint, we need to build it. For every artist in data.json, make one card or row of a playlist using your design from the previous level.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d632c65c-9e48-49d4-b253-32faa74f8be8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6cc520cf-4bc7-4af2-a71d-49b532f76f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "224e4003-9f72-41b0-aa72-2378d0fb1d20",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 6 to complete Levels 6 and 7.\n- Level 6: Students build a web app to display their music playlist or card view.\n- Level 7: Students adjust their loop logic and add safeguards to handle the new JSON structure.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for students struggling with the loop syntax. Make sure they use the correct variable names inside the loop. Ensure students are choosing keys that make sense for their app. For example, if they are building a card view, they need the image url.\n",
        "progression_name": "Design Your Web App"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "224e4003-9f72-41b0-aa72-2378d0fb1d20",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6cc520cf-4bc7-4af2-a71d-49b532f76f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a72e0519-9352-4601-b6ba-c46b0fef6987",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Debug the Code",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that the key name in the JSON must match exactly what they call in their code, including case sensitivity, or the data won't show up."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Check if students have successfully fixed issues with the updated JSON structure by assessing their code in Level 7. Compare their final web previews in Levels 6 and 7 which should look relatively similar."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a72e0519-9352-4601-b6ba-c46b0fef6987",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6cc520cf-4bc7-4af2-a71d-49b532f76f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "688a1292-5dd7-44b4-b288-75f749b470ba",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "688a1292-5dd7-44b4-b288-75f749b470ba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6cc520cf-4bc7-4af2-a71d-49b532f76f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "35263ca8-97c3-4965-aa3f-c3feb201cc38",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *When you make an API call and get back a JSON response, how can you design your code so it still works if a property is different than expected?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should understand that they can design their code to check whether a property exists before using it, provide default or fallback values if something is missing, and avoid hard-coding property names without validation. That way, even if the backend engineers change a field name or structure, their app won’t crash — it will handle the change gracefully and still look good to the user.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "35263ca8-97c3-4965-aa3f-c3feb201cc38",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6cc520cf-4bc7-4af2-a71d-49b532f76f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "84b74dab-d941-4bbe-b329-f8d4714cfecc",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Sometimes the data we get from a server isn't perfect. It might be messy, in the wrong format, or missing entirely. Your job as a developer is to make the app look good anyway!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "84b74dab-d941-4bbe-b329-f8d4714cfecc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6cc520cf-4bc7-4af2-a71d-49b532f76f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2dec45b0-1f8b-4b31-9d5c-96381a223fa0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2dec45b0-1f8b-4b31-9d5c-96381a223fa0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "32b1184d-1a2c-4eca-b07b-1cd4928d83fe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9d6d339f-7ecc-49fa-8f79-ed03fdecfab4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 8 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.\n",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9d6d339f-7ecc-49fa-8f79-ed03fdecfab4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "32b1184d-1a2c-4eca-b07b-1cd4928d83fe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d20c9198-d297-415a-b496-d4adb4228888",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students observe how different apps handle system failures and consider the impact on user experience."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d20c9198-d297-415a-b496-d4adb4228888",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7d4804d0-62ca-41bc-812c-ade8f5317a54"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "46676223-1773-4818-9051-b45c79f32c8e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We've all seen screens like these. Whether it's a 'dinosaur' game when the Wi-Fi drops or a '404' message on a website, these screens are a developer's way of handling a problem. Today, we're going to consider the user experience when a system failure occurs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "46676223-1773-4818-9051-b45c79f32c8e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7d4804d0-62ca-41bc-812c-ade8f5317a54"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6b0edb45-309e-4284-8fa2-998f7b29ec83",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How helpful is the error message?*\n- *How did the page make you feel (calm, confused, or blamed)?*\n- *What is one improvement that you would make?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Have students reflect on whether the cause of failure is clear or not. They should recognize that clear communication and a helpful next step (like a 'Back to homepage' button) maintain user trust even when things break.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to think about the 'vibe' of each page. Is it playful and apologetic, or cold and technical? This sets the stage for understanding Human Centered Reliability."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6b0edb45-309e-4284-8fa2-998f7b29ec83",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7d4804d0-62ca-41bc-812c-ade8f5317a54"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6b495b92-a5e7-4c5a-bb9c-980ee53ca07d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6b495b92-a5e7-4c5a-bb9c-980ee53ca07d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7d4804d0-62ca-41bc-812c-ade8f5317a54"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a25986ab-8472-4ad9-8a8e-264f263f3f95",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How helpful is the error message?*\n- *How did the page make you feel (calm, confused, or blamed)?*\n- *What is one improvement that you would make?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Have students reflect on whether the cause of failure is clear or not. Push them to think about what they would add or change about this page.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How helpful is the error message?*\n- *How did the page make you feel (calm, confused, or blamed)?*\n- *What is one improvement that you would make?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Have students reflect on whether the cause of failure is clear or not. Listen for mentions of 'confusing technical language' or 'missing next steps'.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a25986ab-8472-4ad9-8a8e-264f263f3f95",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7d4804d0-62ca-41bc-812c-ade8f5317a54"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4ecb467c-66be-46e8-87d2-e7ac8fa310e9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students identify failure types in a real-world ticket app scenario and translate user complaints into technical categories.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the When Things Break activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *network failure*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *server failure*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *invalid data*.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "The Diagnostics"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4ecb467c-66be-46e8-87d2-e7ac8fa310e9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "725e5e85-31d8-4a5d-9343-73e812e68a4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f85ae7c8-350e-408e-b2d0-9ca179ae8920",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine you're trying to buy tickets for your favorite band, and the app just spins forever. Frustrating, right? Today, we’ll act as the engineers behind a concert ticket app. Right now, our app is fragile and doesn’t load consistently. We're going to learn how to diagnose these failures and build a more resilient app.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f85ae7c8-350e-408e-b2d0-9ca179ae8920",
        "lesson_activity.key": "725e5e85-31d8-4a5d-9343-73e812e68a4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "73fd6ace-d865-4a3c-97f2-0049c0220a7c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the three main types of failures students will encounter.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 1.\n- Level 1: Students read three specific user complaints and identify if they are Network, Server, or Data failures. Have them complete both sections of their activity guide.\n- Level 2: Students read the definition and example of network failure.\n- Level 3: Students write their own definition of server failure and include a real-world example.\n- Level 4: Students read the definition and example of server failure.\n- Level 5: Students write their own definition of network failure and include a real-world example.\n- Level 6: Students read the definition and example of invalid data.\n- Level 7: Students write their own definition of invalid data and include a real-world example.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who might confuse a slow server with a network failure. As students complete the “Considering the User Experience” section, remind students that “user trust” is the primary metric here. Ask: 'Which choice makes the user feel more in control and less confused?'\n",
        "progression_name": "Explore Error Handling"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "73fd6ace-d865-4a3c-97f2-0049c0220a7c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "725e5e85-31d8-4a5d-9343-73e812e68a4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b3638ae4-74c5-4e66-82a6-05487d81fe10",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Network Failure"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b3638ae4-74c5-4e66-82a6-05487d81fe10",
        "lesson_activity.key": "725e5e85-31d8-4a5d-9343-73e812e68a4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "341130af-528a-48d7-84e9-813e5b0877f1",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Server Failure"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "341130af-528a-48d7-84e9-813e5b0877f1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "725e5e85-31d8-4a5d-9343-73e812e68a4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e56fc63e-9d8a-4d44-876b-aa2fb957ef8e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Invalid Data",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider displaying the previous slide as students complete the “Explore Error Handling” section of their activity guide."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e56fc63e-9d8a-4d44-876b-aa2fb957ef8e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "725e5e85-31d8-4a5d-9343-73e812e68a4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b7f264e9-f652-4ece-b267-bb92b3386faf",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b7f264e9-f652-4ece-b267-bb92b3386faf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "725e5e85-31d8-4a5d-9343-73e812e68a4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a65deaef-2911-413d-8520-b310d3593ca9",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students weigh the trade-offs between showing partial data and blocking access to ensure user trust.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Considering the User Experience"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a65deaef-2911-413d-8520-b310d3593ca9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "725e5e85-31d8-4a5d-9343-73e812e68a4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a168966-3bfe-4079-aab7-2f5b95f11e70",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine you’re now the website developer. You have a tough choice. If you show the page without seats, users might think it's sold out. If you block it, they can't even see the band info. What do you do?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a168966-3bfe-4079-aab7-2f5b95f11e70",
        "lesson_activity.key": "725e5e85-31d8-4a5d-9343-73e812e68a4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "66bd6dd6-8979-4c62-955f-4fadfdf51082",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *The page loaded, but the seat map API is down. Would you choose to show the page with missing data or block off the page entirely?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Have students explain whether they chose Fail Fast or Fail Quietly in their activity guide. Students should recognize that there is no 'perfect' answer, only trade-offs. If students struggle, ask: 'Which choice makes the app look more broken?'\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that 'User Trust' is the primary metric here. Ask: 'Which choice makes the user feel more in control and less confused?'"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Check for justifications that connect the technical strategy back to the human experience (e.g., 'Failing fast prevents the user from trying to buy a seat that isn't there')."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "66bd6dd6-8979-4c62-955f-4fadfdf51082",
        "lesson_activity.key": "725e5e85-31d8-4a5d-9343-73e812e68a4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b1f2115-4f8b-45bd-ba64-bbd81ab424a9",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b1f2115-4f8b-45bd-ba64-bbd81ab424a9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "725e5e85-31d8-4a5d-9343-73e812e68a4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "01b85051-ef4e-42c3-8ef6-9471a93f8971",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students design user-facing solutions for technical failures using a reliability checklist and wireframe sketches."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "01b85051-ef4e-42c3-8ef6-9471a93f8971",
        "lesson_activity.key": "725e5e85-31d8-4a5d-9343-73e812e68a4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1ea8cddf-332c-49d7-adb3-6b0abc2688aa",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now it's time to design the fix. You've diagnosed the problem, now what does the user actually see? Decide if you want the page to fail fast or fail quickly and design an appropriate solution.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1ea8cddf-332c-49d7-adb3-6b0abc2688aa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "725e5e85-31d8-4a5d-9343-73e812e68a4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5471bb8f-88f7-4ef7-af1b-0b1de74b1747",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 8 to design the UI for a types of failures.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for 'The Action'. Every error message should give the user something to do (Retry, Refresh, Contact Support). If a student just says 'It broke', ask them: 'What action should the user take now?'\n",
        "progression_name": "Designing Recovery States",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider distributing the (Optional) Designing for Failure activity guide for students to sketch out their ideas on paper."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Review students’ designs. A successful design includes non-technical language, a clear next step, and a 'calm' tone rather than an alarming or highly technical one."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5471bb8f-88f7-4ef7-af1b-0b1de74b1747",
        "lesson_activity.key": "725e5e85-31d8-4a5d-9343-73e812e68a4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5c299709-e535-44bb-8c31-f44e8fe89649",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5c299709-e535-44bb-8c31-f44e8fe89649",
        "lesson_activity.key": "725e5e85-31d8-4a5d-9343-73e812e68a4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ef4c2c0-a396-4e4f-a45a-c420e0e83b7d",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How does a well-designed error message build trust even when an app is broken?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students understand that transparency and guidance maintain the relationship between the user and the software. Trust is built when the app \"takes responsibility\" for the error and offers a path forward.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ef4c2c0-a396-4e4f-a45a-c420e0e83b7d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "725e5e85-31d8-4a5d-9343-73e812e68a4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "677d1c6a-4d73-4ba3-8795-963e92c5f7da",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "677d1c6a-4d73-4ba3-8795-963e92c5f7da",
        "lesson_activity.key": "63924507-a1d9-4fe4-bbfa-d50eb2ba9741"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7bcc8b2d-67bf-441d-85ab-a9425b31a4df",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 9 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7bcc8b2d-67bf-441d-85ab-a9425b31a4df",
        "lesson_activity.key": "63924507-a1d9-4fe4-bbfa-d50eb2ba9741"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "68263eaf-2a0a-40aa-8d8d-1b1aa591a0a2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students analyze how different failure experiences affect user trust and begin recognizing that good apps are designed to handle errors intentionally, not accidentally.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Rank the three failure screens from most to least trustworthy. Explain your ranking.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that how a system fails matters as much as whether it fails. A friendly fallback communicates planning and care, a blank screen communicates neglect or instability, and exposed technical errors communicate lack of user-centered design. Students should begin to see trust as a product of intentional error handling.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *When nothing appears on the screen, what questions start running through your head as a user?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify that a blank screen creates uncertainty and loss of control. Without feedback, users may blame themselves, their device, or the app. Students should understand that silence is not neutral, it is a communication failure, and that good systems provide feedback even when something goes wrong.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What message does this error screen send about the reliability of the system?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that exposing raw technical errors reduces perceived reliability and trust. Even if the issue is minor, showing stack traces or code suggests poor planning and lack of audience awareness. Students should understand that error messages must match the user’s level of understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why does the cat screen make the failure feel less frustrating, even though the problem isn't actually fixed?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that tone, visuals, and clear messaging regulate user emotion and preserve trust. A fallback does not fix the underlying technical problem, but it protects the user experience by communicating awareness and offering guidance. Students should connect this to intentional design through try/catch.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "68263eaf-2a0a-40aa-8d8d-1b1aa591a0a2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "77afe04c-fc3b-4048-ac48-e0cf8c58ea86"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c4b8c306-fdb7-4806-a1ea-742c1d9496d8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students learn to wrap asynchronous fetch logic in try and catch blocks to handle errors gracefully and maintain user trust."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c4b8c306-fdb7-4806-a1ea-742c1d9496d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "84c6ebed-1a8f-4216-a950-58c22d144568",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Fetching data from an API is like a trapeze act. Usually, the artist catches the bar—that's our data—and the show goes on. But sometimes, things go wrong. The bar is slippery because the data is bad, or the rope snaps because the WiFi is out. Without a safety net, the show ends immediately in a crash. Today, we're going to learn how to build that safety net using try and catch.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "84c6ebed-1a8f-4216-a950-58c22d144568",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "25d13d46-8c00-4153-8afc-d6a45cb0fc46",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the trapeze metaphor for fetching data.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use this metaphor to emphasize that errors aren't always the programmer's fault—sometimes the \"rope\" (network) just breaks, and we have to be ready for it."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "25d13d46-8c00-4153-8afc-d6a45cb0fc46",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ced62016-bc52-455c-b9bc-f0c0d3da75e7",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ced62016-bc52-455c-b9bc-f0c0d3da75e7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d4ab74b0-9f73-4721-a06a-3a9f7633eaca",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the try block.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that the code inside the try block is the \"dangerous stunt.\" We only put code there that has a chance of failing, like an API request."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d4ab74b0-9f73-4721-a06a-3a9f7633eaca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "474a818e-c3f1-4ae2-92ef-204b09599d48",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "474a818e-c3f1-4ae2-92ef-204b09599d48",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd8d99ef-f724-45b7-8968-aee388b50e06",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the catch block."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd8d99ef-f724-45b7-8968-aee388b50e06",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e4f5328-7f08-4a39-946d-37d3573f4b9f",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If the try block is the stunt, the catch block is the safety net. It's waiting right below. If everything goes perfectly, the net is never used. But if there's a fall, the catch block grabs the error so the whole app doesn't hit the ground.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e4f5328-7f08-4a39-946d-37d3573f4b9f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "246aa1a9-25b9-45fb-a190-e2dcfaee5011",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the syntax of a try...catch block."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "246aa1a9-25b9-45fb-a190-e2dcfaee5011",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6e915d81-113f-4bd4-bda8-12fba08b2ee9",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Notice the curly braces. Everything inside the 'try' curly braces is protected. If any line in there fails, the computer immediately jumps down to the 'catch' block. It ignores the rest of the 'try' code and runs the safety instructions instead. This is a professional concept called Defensive Design. It means you don't just code for when things go right; you code for when things go wrong. By handling errors gracefully, you keep your users from feeling frustrated or scared that your app is broken or unsafe.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Point out the (error) next to catch. Explain that this is a variable that holds the details of what went wrong, though we don't always have to show it to the user."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6e915d81-113f-4bd4-bda8-12fba08b2ee9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "576e84c3-f2e2-4d01-8714-f69bbb77f1f4",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "576e84c3-f2e2-4d01-8714-f69bbb77f1f4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a65ab6d-f426-4fbd-8c34-102802145380",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *fallback*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 4.\n- Level 1: Students read the formal definition of try/catch.\n- Level 2: Students write a personal definition for try/catch and provide one example.\n- Level 3: Students read the formal definition of fallback.\n- Level 4: Students write a personal definition for fallback and provide one example.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** On Level 2, check that students can point to which code belongs in the try block and what goes in catch (error). Ask probing questions like: What “dangerous stunt” is your try block protecting? and What will the user see if catch runs? On Level 4, look for examples that clearly show something helpful to the user (apology, image, retry) instead of doing nothing or exposing raw error text Ensure students describe fallbacks as planned backup experiences, not random glitches.\n",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: try...catch"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a65ab6d-f426-4fbd-8c34-102802145380",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d6c52e3c-9f37-4b87-8576-201fef55cae1",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Fallback",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Look for explanations that mention both “trying” risky code and “catching” errors to prevent crashes. Flag students who only talk about “fixing bugs” without mentioning what shows on screen."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d6c52e3c-9f37-4b87-8576-201fef55cae1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5ee7c169-4224-4cce-a3ad-473d91cb3453",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5ee7c169-4224-4cce-a3ad-473d91cb3453",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5850cb14-9620-49dd-8bdd-363a61e6820e",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Imagine a login attempt fails. What should the system show the user, and why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students will evaluate how different error-handling strategies influence user experience, trust, and system design tradeoffs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 5 to complete Levels 5 through 6.\n- Level 5: Students predict and then run code with a broken URL to see it crash.\n- Level 6: Students modify the code by wrapping the fetch in a try block and adding a catch block that prints to the console.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who forget the curly braces for the catch block. Remind them that catch needs its own block of code to run. Ask: \"Now that you've added the catch, does the red error still stop the whole program?\"\n",
        "progression_name": "Predict and Run: Broken Fetch"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5850cb14-9620-49dd-8bdd-363a61e6820e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "79e4b3e2-74cd-43c0-82a5-94e7630aafed",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Modify: Broken Fetch",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "In Level 6, the code should successfully print the \"Caught you!\" message instead of crashing the console."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "79e4b3e2-74cd-43c0-82a5-94e7630aafed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9ba48212-9b48-48fb-9b76-791dfee75a58",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9ba48212-9b48-48fb-9b76-791dfee75a58",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "202a8ef2-d271-4650-8e4d-cce75ae39367",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How does using try and catch make your app more trustworthy for users?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that even if a feature fails, a controlled error message maintains user trust better than a crash or technical jargon. Even if the data doesn't load, a polite message or fallback image keeps the user feeling safe and in control.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Chaos Challenge scenarios.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "202a8ef2-d271-4650-8e4d-cce75ae39367",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "20629c02-6321-41f4-ae63-41a0632a5b5b",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It's Launch Day, and everything is going wrong! The API is down, and users are starting to complain. You are the Crisis Manager. You need to choose one of three problems and fix it so our users don't abandon the app.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Briefly explain the three options: 1. Fixing 'Tech Speak' (The Apology), 2. Adding a 'Fallback' image, or 3. Adding a 'Retry' button. Encourage students to pick the one that sounds most interesting to them."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "20629c02-6321-41f4-ae63-41a0632a5b5b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "45800fba-311e-4e49-a0d1-1bc5c95184af",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 7 to complete Levels 7 through 8.\n- Level 7: Students read the three \"Chaos\" scenarios.\n- Level 8A: Students write code to display a polite, non-technical apology message.\n- Level 8B: Students write code to display a local fallback image when the fetch fails.\n- Level 8C: Student write code to wire up a \"Try Again\" button to the fetch function.\n",
        "progression_name": "Chaos Challenge Introduction"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "45800fba-311e-4e49-a0d1-1bc5c95184af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "857d6798-659a-4ac5-bc90-d22c29d49fd7",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Chaos Challenge",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Check that students have placed their fallback logic (the apology, image, or button) inside the catch block, not the try block."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "857d6798-659a-4ac5-bc90-d22c29d49fd7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96d4d783-8667-4619-84e4-6f8e9222620a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ac041b5c-8ff5-4ce8-b163-b514673e6eba",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ac041b5c-8ff5-4ce8-b163-b514673e6eba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e5cc3552-d840-40f8-b5b8-223cfa10484f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5707ed9d-6a8a-4c78-ac59-42c538471258",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 9 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5707ed9d-6a8a-4c78-ac59-42c538471258",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e5cc3552-d840-40f8-b5b8-223cfa10484f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b0a1356-7b3f-4f69-864c-47fd8051bcc7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students engage with the concept of loading states and how visual cues set user expectations."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b0a1356-7b3f-4f69-864c-47fd8051bcc7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1945eedc-0a01-46bf-8969-bf5b1513d9a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e1ba3720-6861-41ea-a442-b83945bb4e3f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We're going to play a guessing game. I am going to show you a loading screen and you have to describe what you think is about to appear on the screen.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e1ba3720-6861-41ea-a442-b83945bb4e3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1945eedc-0a01-46bf-8969-bf5b1513d9a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1631a429-1d16-4b88-99bd-f51fe232b877",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you think is loading here? How do you know?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify that the shape and layout of the gray boxes (the 'skeleton') mimics the layout of a social media profile. Students should realize that even without text, certain shapes suggest specific types of content. Because gray boxes tell your brain what to expect, you aren't staring at a blank screen; you're looking at a layout that is about to be filled.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Push students to go beyond just saying the app is loading. Have them be specific about what kind of content they would expect to see. If students struggle, ask them to think about the apps they use most often."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1631a429-1d16-4b88-99bd-f51fe232b877",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1945eedc-0a01-46bf-8969-bf5b1513d9a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a774e69c-4e7b-416e-84ae-b840aa9cdba0",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a774e69c-4e7b-416e-84ae-b840aa9cdba0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1945eedc-0a01-46bf-8969-bf5b1513d9a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "729702ff-87b7-49ae-91ef-25df992ec4e8",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What do you think is loading here?*\n- *Why include text as well as a progress bar?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students understand that the progress bar manages patience by showing time remaining, while the text communicates that the app is actively working on specific tasks behind the scenes. Without the bar, you don't know how long it will take. Without the text, you don't know if the app is actually doing anything useful.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Point out that this loading screen also features a preview of the costumes that users will likely see on the loaded screen. This helps users anticipate what they are about to see and manages their expectations for waiting time."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "729702ff-87b7-49ae-91ef-25df992ec4e8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1945eedc-0a01-46bf-8969-bf5b1513d9a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "83b3ce62-5b6e-459a-8e19-88fbf92a3651",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "83b3ce62-5b6e-459a-8e19-88fbf92a3651",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1945eedc-0a01-46bf-8969-bf5b1513d9a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2513cc26-7c12-4495-a779-692502ed2fc5",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What do you think is loading here?*\n- *Why does this represent poor design?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Introduce that poor design can lead to low user trust. Students should realize that without context, the user has no clue what is happening behind the scenes, how long it will take, or what to expect when the page loads. Students should understand that it is best to communicate clearly so users aren't left guessing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2513cc26-7c12-4495-a779-692502ed2fc5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1945eedc-0a01-46bf-8969-bf5b1513d9a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2f879e61-6391-42e5-ab14-1215e65fb953",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students design loading, empty, and error states for a web app by deciding on text and visual assets..\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define human-centered reliability.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Design Phase"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2f879e61-6391-42e5-ab14-1215e65fb953",
        "lesson_activity.key": "85cd263e-43b0-4dc0-b5c9-0bcae1ed5d25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "85291c1c-1770-418f-8404-cbc0fc1f06cd",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Every time your app makes a request to an API, it needs to handle these three scenarios. You never want to leave a user looking at a blank screen or a broken page. Handling these states is what makes an app feel professional and reliable.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "85291c1c-1770-418f-8404-cbc0fc1f06cd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "85cd263e-43b0-4dc0-b5c9-0bcae1ed5d25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93a21f38-bfdf-4023-bc04-5e283c1f4dbb",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain loading state, empty state, and error state. Remind students that loading the page perfectly, i.e. following the “Happy Path, is only one possible outcome for the user experience. Reliability comes from handling the other paths well so the user never feels confused or upset.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "An empty state isn't the same as an error state. A good empty state tells the user why there is no data being displayed. An error state communicates that something went wrong and the user will need to try again."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93a21f38-bfdf-4023-bc04-5e283c1f4dbb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "85cd263e-43b0-4dc0-b5c9-0bcae1ed5d25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "307eba27-d43a-4c85-b85c-3d52b96c4f27",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "307eba27-d43a-4c85-b85c-3d52b96c4f27",
        "lesson_activity.key": "85cd263e-43b0-4dc0-b5c9-0bcae1ed5d25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af323257-c72b-497b-8036-53bbf487da20",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students implement the logic for loading, empty, and error states in Web Lab using the assets they designed.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 5.\n- Level 1: Students read the definition for human-centered reliability.\n- Level 2: Students write their own definition of human-centered reliability.\n- Level 3: Students use Sketch Lab to design their three states.\n- Level 4: Students implement their loading, empty, and error states in Web Lab and test their work.\n- Level 5: Students test a partner’s app by forcing the data array to be empty and checking if their \"vibe\" holds up.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are correctly placing their innerHTML updates before the fetch call begins (for the loading state).\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Build Phase",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Human-Centered Reliability"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af323257-c72b-497b-8036-53bbf487da20",
        "lesson_activity.key": "85cd263e-43b0-4dc0-b5c9-0bcae1ed5d25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "17bafc16-b588-4fb5-aea6-8bc0bca4a292",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Drafting the Web App State"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "17bafc16-b588-4fb5-aea6-8bc0bca4a292",
        "lesson_activity.key": "85cd263e-43b0-4dc0-b5c9-0bcae1ed5d25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "24f7de64-356e-43ee-8a64-5a991e1d44e2",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Build Your Web App State"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "24f7de64-356e-43ee-8a64-5a991e1d44e2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "85cd263e-43b0-4dc0-b5c9-0bcae1ed5d25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "05e5441d-42aa-4927-9779-d37857b52a7c",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Getting Feedback",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The program on Level 3 is designed to choose an outcome randomly. Make sure students keep clicking \"Find flights\" and resetting the results until they have tested all three states."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Check student responses on Level 2. An exemplar response explains that good communication builds trust and prevents users from guessing what the app is doing. On Level 4, check that students have successfully implemented a visible loading indicator that appears as soon as the button is clicked. Check that students have implemented an empty state and error state."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "05e5441d-42aa-4927-9779-d37857b52a7c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "85cd263e-43b0-4dc0-b5c9-0bcae1ed5d25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b64f70c8-2917-4847-8956-391ccb1979f6",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b64f70c8-2917-4847-8956-391ccb1979f6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "85cd263e-43b0-4dc0-b5c9-0bcae1ed5d25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4590b9e8-adef-4bf6-9a90-6c71e3d56c1d",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does seeing a loading message impact your trust in an app?*\n- *What happens if an app shows nothing when a search has no results?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that clear communication prevents users from assuming the app is broken. Loading states tell the user that the data is loading and not to navigate away from the page. Empty states communicate that no data matches the search criteria, rather than leaving users confused by seeing a blank screen.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4590b9e8-adef-4bf6-9a90-6c71e3d56c1d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "85cd263e-43b0-4dc0-b5c9-0bcae1ed5d25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "740b5334-8292-4bec-8b6f-97eb97503977",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "740b5334-8292-4bec-8b6f-97eb97503977",
        "lesson_activity.key": "112f644d-a218-4eaa-bbc2-901e4d51d770"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8dd68ec4-394a-4588-a80e-247112a3f8b9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 6 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8dd68ec4-394a-4588-a80e-247112a3f8b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "112f644d-a218-4eaa-bbc2-901e4d51d770"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bb3bfa6a-bd1e-4a67-982b-13a1bb690682",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students build an intuitive understanding of the tradeoffs between local and live data sources."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bb3bfa6a-bd1e-4a67-982b-13a1bb690682",
        "lesson_activity.key": "499bd54a-190e-4bd5-b397-6a67b94a5554"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5758f7c4-ec2d-4046-b567-f782ef145a9a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When we build apps, we make decisions about where data lives. We can hardcode it directly into the app, or fetch it from the internet. Today, you’ll act as developers making that decision.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5758f7c4-ec2d-4046-b567-f782ef145a9a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "499bd54a-190e-4bd5-b397-6a67b94a5554"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "239a7d46-6e0d-4163-8d81-1e4553a8c344",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** You're building an app. For each item, decide: Local (hardcoded) or Live (fetched):\n- *Live NBA Game Scores*\n- *Home Icon Image*\n- *List of Music Genres (Rock, Pop, Jazz)*\n- *Weather Forecast*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that frequently changing, time-sensitive data should be Live, while stable structural elements are better stored locally.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What pattern do you notice in the solution below?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify that frequently changing, time-sensitive data should be fetched Live, while stable, structural, or rarely changing data is better stored locally for speed and reliability.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Why not make the Home icon Live?*\n- *Why not make the NBA scores Local?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students articulate why developers choose Local data for stability and instant load times, and Live data for real-time updates and dynamic content.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "239a7d46-6e0d-4163-8d81-1e4553a8c344",
        "lesson_activity.key": "499bd54a-190e-4bd5-b397-6a67b94a5554"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d909dbd3-6686-4169-bfb3-88851c7d766e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will distinguish between live and local data and explain why professional apps use both for speed and reliability.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Mini-Lesson: Live vs Local Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d909dbd3-6686-4169-bfb3-88851c7d766e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a83aefa0-1830-4afb-a1fb-b6461bb6fc09"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "83a1484f-2eb2-4173-aac6-af7c00c2e316",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Every app you use relies on data. The real question is: where does that data come from, and what happens if it’s not available?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "83a1484f-2eb2-4173-aac6-af7c00c2e316",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a83aefa0-1830-4afb-a1fb-b6461bb6fc09"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5c95eb98-24ed-4346-970f-79f55330743f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *live data*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *local data*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What is the biggest risk of relying ONLY on live data for your app?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify that if the API goes down or the user is offline, the app will be empty or broken (the \"White Screen of Death\").\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5c95eb98-24ed-4346-970f-79f55330743f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a83aefa0-1830-4afb-a1fb-b6461bb6fc09"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7fc6927-a82a-45b6-bf4f-765b1138130c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When we build professional apps, we don't just use live data. We also use local data for two very specific jobs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7fc6927-a82a-45b6-bf4f-765b1138130c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a83aefa0-1830-4afb-a1fb-b6461bb6fc09"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "53f1b43e-8bed-4d68-96e3-e80eeb71cbcf",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain why we use static constants for UI speed and the safety role of fallback data."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "53f1b43e-8bed-4d68-96e3-e80eeb71cbcf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a83aefa0-1830-4afb-a1fb-b6461bb6fc09"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b77d34f8-437b-43b5-bd17-29e3e11e20a5",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine opening an app and seeing nothing but a blank screen for five seconds while it waits for a server. Static constants let us \"paint\" the app's structure instantly while the live data is still fetching in the background.\n\nAPIs are powerful, but they are also fragile. A server might go down, or a user's Wi-Fi might drop and backup data is our \"Safety Net\" that keeps the app functional even when things go wrong.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that backup data must have the exact same structure (the same keys like \"title\" or \"image\") as the real API data. If they don't match, the code will break when it switches to the backup."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b77d34f8-437b-43b5-bd17-29e3e11e20a5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a83aefa0-1830-4afb-a1fb-b6461bb6fc09"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3129ca55-d226-4671-98c2-86cb021c7b7a",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 4.\n- Level 1: Students read the formal definition of local data.\n- Level 2: Students write a personal definition for local data and provide one example.\n- Level 3: Students read the formal definition of live data.\n- Level 4: Students write a personal definition for live data and provide one example.\n",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Local Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3129ca55-d226-4671-98c2-86cb021c7b7a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a83aefa0-1830-4afb-a1fb-b6461bb6fc09"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0c5b29d9-a728-4ebe-ac44-e5abff8f694b",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Live Data",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Have students test their definitions against a real example (e.g., a weather app or a saved photo) to check whether they truly understand the difference between local and live data."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0c5b29d9-a728-4ebe-ac44-e5abff8f694b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a83aefa0-1830-4afb-a1fb-b6461bb6fc09"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6de4904-429c-4d21-a58f-ba322087fa52",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6de4904-429c-4d21-a58f-ba322087fa52",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a83aefa0-1830-4afb-a1fb-b6461bb6fc09"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6671a7e0-b5b3-4287-a7d1-83b596014386",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How would you decide what data to always load as a static constant? What data should be used as a backup?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students understand that core interface elements and essential structure should be static and reliable, while external or frequently changing data should be dynamic with a fallback to preserve usability.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6671a7e0-b5b3-4287-a7d1-83b596014386",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a83aefa0-1830-4afb-a1fb-b6461bb6fc09"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af5bebf6-458b-4b1c-a1c7-09a7ad16666c",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will design a resilient “hybrid” web app that combines static configuration, live API data, and local backup data, demonstrating how real-world systems handle both dynamic content and failure conditions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 5 to complete Levels 5 through 7.\n- Level 5: Initialize the static UI using an appConfig object.\n- Level 6: Create the backupData array to match the API structure.\n- Level 7: Implement try/catch logic to switch to backup data if the fetch fails.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "The Hybrid Architecture",
        "progression_name": "Create an App Shell"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af5bebf6-458b-4b1c-a1c7-09a7ad16666c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a83aefa0-1830-4afb-a1fb-b6461bb6fc09"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1e32798a-d537-41d8-bc21-457e06d117ad",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Store Backup Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1e32798a-d537-41d8-bc21-457e06d117ad",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a83aefa0-1830-4afb-a1fb-b6461bb6fc09"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c35faf51-d0b5-4d3c-8de5-6864dba351ac",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Implement Hybrid Logic",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 7 is the culmination. Check that the app renders the static shell immediately and falls back to backupData when the fetch fails."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c35faf51-d0b5-4d3c-8de5-6864dba351ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a83aefa0-1830-4afb-a1fb-b6461bb6fc09"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e18c35bf-ddaf-4328-8bac-167392ecdff0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e18c35bf-ddaf-4328-8bac-167392ecdff0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "710872c8-a42c-4126-bb7d-7c97f93b408b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a1fec8b7-6ef5-44a2-8b95-08d84900c679",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 8 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a1fec8b7-6ef5-44a2-8b95-08d84900c679",
        "lesson_activity.key": "710872c8-a42c-4126-bb7d-7c97f93b408b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bff8580b-4f53-476a-bd95-c4cdc78fddc1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What makes an app feel fast and responsive?*\n- *What makes an app feel broken or confusing?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students begin thinking about responsiveness, feedback, and user trust — which sets up async + loading states.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bff8580b-4f53-476a-bd95-c4cdc78fddc1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccbaf747-5c20-4b93-84d3-08fd7d0f11e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dde1b376-ff61-4433-935d-38cc2a4224db",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Explore-Style App Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through the simplified project goals. Explain that their app must fetch live data from an API, render it dynamically, add interactive reveal features, and clearly communicate loading, error, and empty states. Emphasize that reliability and user trust are central to the project — their design decisions should ensure the app never feels frozen, broken, or confusing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to read the rubric on the last two pages of the project planning guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you notice about the project requirements and rubric? What does success look like for this project?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify that success involves more than just making the app work. Listen for themes like reliable API integration, clear interaction design, effective loading and error handling, thoughtful documentation, and intentional AI use. Students should recognize that a strong project demonstrates both technical accuracy and user trust.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the milestone structure of the project. Explain that the Explore-Style App Project is divided into four milestones, each with its own goals and deliverables. Use the visual roadmap to preview the journey students will take — from choosing an API to handling edge cases.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that milestones help them manage time and track progress, just like in real design and development projects. Encourage them to think of each milestone as a \"checkpoint\" to reflect on what's working, what needs improvement, and how their design aligns with their user and purpose."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dde1b376-ff61-4433-935d-38cc2a4224db",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccbaf747-5c20-4b93-84d3-08fd7d0f11e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1e0934c9-5ac5-4414-b77f-39961f7db429",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1e0934c9-5ac5-4414-b77f-39961f7db429",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccbaf747-5c20-4b93-84d3-08fd7d0f11e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "64ffa83f-04a4-442d-a68d-d6b9e6d9a037",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that today’s focus is Milestone 1: Connect to an API & Build Your Dynamic List. Students will choose an API, test the endpoint, identify meaningful fields, and render a working dynamic list using async and await. Direct students to open their Explore-Style App Project Guide and locate the Milestone 1 section. Have them read through the Goals and Deliverables so they understand what must be completed by the end of the milestone.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to highlight or note any deliverables that feel exciting or challenging. This helps you gauge where support may be needed. Remind students that Milestone 1 focuses on building a reliable API connection and rendering live data correctly — not on styling or advanced features yet. The priority is a working fetch, clear field choices, and a functional dynamic list."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "64ffa83f-04a4-442d-a68d-d6b9e6d9a037",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccbaf747-5c20-4b93-84d3-08fd7d0f11e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cd5c0dc2-b057-4d1a-b40d-943c48b105f3",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 1 to begin Milestone 1: Connect to an API & Build Your Dynamic List. Students should use the support tools to choose and test an API, inspect the JSON structure, select meaningful fields, and plan their card layout before fully building their render logic.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work in the hub, ask guiding questions such as:\n- Which API are you using, and why did you choose it?\n- What fields will appear in your summary card?\n- Does your JSON response match the fields you plan to display?\n- Where does async and await fit into your fetch logic?\n\nUse these check-ins to confirm students are testing their API connection and planning their data flow before layering on additional features.",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to inspect the JSON carefully and sketch their card layout before focusing on full implementation. Understanding the structure first will prevent undefined errors later."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Set expectations for pacing. Milestone 1 can typically be completed by the end of class or early next class. Emphasize that a working API connection and clean dynamic list matter more than visual polish at this stage."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cd5c0dc2-b057-4d1a-b40d-943c48b105f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccbaf747-5c20-4b93-84d3-08fd7d0f11e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b5243cc3-2f3d-4ec4-bb34-19a68c400c86",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b5243cc3-2f3d-4ec4-bb34-19a68c400c86",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccbaf747-5c20-4b93-84d3-08fd7d0f11e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93f6b2dd-9aa8-4b63-8dff-62f7abfd3b6b",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Milestone 1 Reflection section in their project planning guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are discussing and sharing these reflection questions with a thought partner.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few volunteers to share aloud or conduct a quick pair-and-share to close."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93f6b2dd-9aa8-4b63-8dff-62f7abfd3b6b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccbaf747-5c20-4b93-84d3-08fd7d0f11e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "83266ff1-cb46-44b2-92e4-b5212847fdd1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts:\n- *When you use an app like Instagram, Amazon, or Spotify, why don’t they show all information at once?*\n- *What makes a “See More” or “View Details” interaction helpful?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that hiding and revealing information improves clarity, reduces overload, and helps users focus. This prepares them to think intentionally about what belongs in a summary card versus a detail view.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "83266ff1-cb46-44b2-92e4-b5212847fdd1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "610d0d73-ce8d-4f90-8781-2da89410a1b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "67ddd525-198b-4ccf-b2cc-7d6144f20c15",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 2 of the project. Explain that today students will focus on completing Milestone 1 and starting work on Milestone 2. Direct students to open their Explore-Style App Project Guide and locate the Milestone 1 and 2 sections. Have them review the Goals and Deliverables for each milestone so they understand what to prioritize during work time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to finish any remaining Milestone 1 work in Level 1 if needed, then move to Level 2 to begin Milestone 2. Explain that today they will shift from building a static dynamic list to making their app interactive. The focus is adding a clear trigger button, attaching event listeners, and planning how additional details will be revealed. Emphasize that they should start simple: get one card interaction working correctly before expanding to the full set.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task and progressing toward a complete first draft. Ask check-in questions like:\n- Where is your “See More” or “Reveal” button placed on the card?\n- How does your click event know which card was selected?\n- What happens when you click one card — does only that card update?\n- Are you toggling hidden content or fetching additional detail data?\n- Does your reveal improve clarity, or does it make the layout confusing?\n\nRemind students that their interaction should be intentional and predictable. If multiple cards update at once or nothing happens, guide them to trace the flow: click → event listener → reveal logic → render. Encourage them to debug using console.log before adding complexity.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 2"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "67ddd525-198b-4ccf-b2cc-7d6144f20c15",
        "lesson_activity.key": "610d0d73-ce8d-4f90-8781-2da89410a1b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d3030340-e079-4d38-8993-c597a0e8844c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Milestone 2 Reflection section in their project planning guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write and discuss, listen for reflections that explain how their reveal interaction improves (or complicates) the user experience. Encourage students to think about whether their button placement, reveal behavior, and detail layout make information clearer or more confusing. Prompt them to identify one specific improvement they would make to strengthen clarity or interaction flow.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few students to share one interaction decision that improved usability. Emphasize that designing clear, predictable interactions is a key part of building trustworthy apps. Recognizing how small choices — like button labels or reveal timing — affect the user experience shows growing design awareness."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d3030340-e079-4d38-8993-c597a0e8844c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "610d0d73-ce8d-4f90-8781-2da89410a1b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "80c706f0-fcff-4fdd-a86e-e30e0b4bcf08",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Looking at your app so far, what might happen if your API takes a long time to load — or doesn’t load at all? Where might your app feel confusing or broken to a user right now?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students anticipate weak points in their current app, especially around loading, errors, or empty results. Listen for awareness that the app may look frozen, blank, or unclear without feedback. This prepares them to intentionally add loading states, try/catch error handling, and empty-result messaging in Milestone 3.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "80c706f0-fcff-4fdd-a86e-e30e0b4bcf08",
        "lesson_activity.key": "39a472ad-7357-4872-a4ac-5cd15489f0bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f128fbf8-3f79-42bb-94b7-956f8b8a2e55",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks for Day 3. Explain that today students will wrap up Milestone 2 and begin Milestone 3. Direct students to open their Explore-Style App Project Guide and locate the Milestone 2 and 3 sections. Have them review the Goals and Deliverables for each milestone so they understand what to prioritize during work time.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 3",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Highlight that the project is shifting from building interaction to strengthening reliability. Students should first ensure their reveal interaction works clearly, then focus on adding loading states, try/catch error handling, and empty-result messaging. Encourage them not to aim for perfection immediately — get one defensive feature working clearly, then refine it through testing and debugging."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f128fbf8-3f79-42bb-94b7-956f8b8a2e55",
        "lesson_activity.key": "39a472ad-7357-4872-a4ac-5cd15489f0bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "192fe8c3-2e20-4e07-ba36-198a0e7fc30b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "192fe8c3-2e20-4e07-ba36-198a0e7fc30b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "39a472ad-7357-4872-a4ac-5cd15489f0bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5b2c48ef-9935-4f03-9db9-a48140caf1c2",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete any remaining Milestone 2 interaction work if needed, then move into Milestone 3. Explain that today’s focus is strengthening their app so it never looks frozen or broken. Students will add loading states, implement try/catch error handling, design clear status messages, and test how their app behaves when something goes wrong. Emphasize that professional apps are not defined by never failing — they are defined by how clearly they handle failure.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are actively implementing and testing defensive features. Ask guiding questions such as:\n- What does the user see immediately after they click a button?\n- Where does your loading state appear?\n- What happens if the API request fails?\n- Does your error message clearly explain what happened?\n- What happens if the API returns zero results?\n- Have you intentionally broken your URL to test your error handling?",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 3",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Reinforce that testing failure is a professional habit, not a mistake. Encourage students to intentionally break their app to see how it responds — change the API URL, simulate empty results, or slow down the request. Remind them that strong developers assume things will fail and design for clarity when they do. Emphasize that an app that communicates clearly during loading or errors builds more trust than one that silently breaks."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5b2c48ef-9935-4f03-9db9-a48140caf1c2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "39a472ad-7357-4872-a4ac-5cd15489f0bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7b45ab05-530d-4f16-a016-8482b52d707a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7b45ab05-530d-4f16-a016-8482b52d707a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "39a472ad-7357-4872-a4ac-5cd15489f0bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "688e3caa-83ef-4610-9455-c5638e17cd9d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Milestone 3 Reflection section in their project planning guide. Let students know they can continue to add on to this reflection as they continue to make progress on Milestone 3 and conduct more testing.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "688e3caa-83ef-4610-9455-c5638e17cd9d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "39a472ad-7357-4872-a4ac-5cd15489f0bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d4753dd-9922-49de-b5d3-207887ec14e8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If someone else had to maintain your app next week, what would they need to understand quickly? What parts of your code might be confusing to someone who didn’t write it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students begin thinking about code clarity, documentation, and ownership. Listen for ideas about readable variable names, comments, API attribution, and clear explanations of async and error logic.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d4753dd-9922-49de-b5d3-207887ec14e8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd0d6140-6f9f-45c1-90df-d3079340a328"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "20f7a2c6-b4ca-40c7-8f52-aa704854b08c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 4 of the project. Explain that today students will focus on Milestones 3 and 4. Direct students to open their Explore-Style App Project Guide and locate the Milestone 3 and 4 sections. Have them review the Goals and Deliverables for each milestone so they understand what to prioritize during work time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to wrap up any remaining Milestone 3 reliability work if needed, then move into Milestone 4. Explain that today’s focus is preparing their app for handoff and submission. Students will create a clear README.md, add meaningful comments explaining their async and error handling logic, and clean up their code. Emphasize that this milestone is about ownership — making sure someone else could understand, maintain, and trust their work.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are documenting and refining their projects thoughtfully. Ask guiding questions such as:\n- Does your README clearly explain what your app does and who it’s for?\n- Have you included a working link to your API source?\n- Do your comments explain why the code works, not just what it does?\n- If someone new read your async function, would they understand the flow?\n- Have you removed unnecessary console.log statements? Does your final version run without visible errors?",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 4",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Frame this as release preparation. Professional developers document their work and clean their code before shipping. Remind students that clear comments and a well-written README demonstrate real understanding. Ownership means being able to explain your decisions, your defensive design, and how your app depends on external APIs."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "20f7a2c6-b4ca-40c7-8f52-aa704854b08c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd0d6140-6f9f-45c1-90df-d3079340a328"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cd765672-87d4-41d1-b931-9657ece88f06",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cd765672-87d4-41d1-b931-9657ece88f06",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd0d6140-6f9f-45c1-90df-d3079340a328"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "402e8fce-8bb7-48dc-87f2-90450d11354f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to revisit the Milestone 4 Reflection section in their project planning guide and add to what they wrote previously. They should update their reflection with any final revisions they made, improvements to clarity or documentation, and insights about ownership and reliability. Encourage them to include specific examples from their app.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that refining their reflection after another round of revision mirrors how professional developers document progress. Encourage them to identify what changed, what improved, and what they understand more clearly now. Highlight that strong reflections include concrete examples — such as how they clarified their async logic, improved an error message, or revised their README — rather than general statements."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "402e8fce-8bb7-48dc-87f2-90450d11354f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd0d6140-6f9f-45c1-90df-d3079340a328"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c9e63ea3-035a-4873-8283-46ca0660bb8c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What is one improvement you made this week that had the biggest impact on your app? What are you most proud of in your project?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students surface growth and intentional decisions. Listen for mentions of async logic, reveal interaction, loading/error states, documentation, or debugging breakthroughs.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c9e63ea3-035a-4873-8283-46ca0660bb8c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4edddc33-e239-4fb0-9a8c-63f2293a81ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af73f2ac-3ae1-469f-90e6-bcecbdde09c9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks for Day 5. Explain that today students will finalize Milestone 4 by polishing their documentation, reviewing their code for clarity, and preparing their app for presentation. Direct students to check their Explore-Style App Project Guide and confirm they have completed all Milestone 4 deliverables before showcasing their work.",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to test their app one final time before presenting. Remind them to check for unusual edge cases, empty results, and loading or error behavior. Make sure their README is complete, their API link works, and their comments clearly explain their logic."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af73f2ac-3ae1-469f-90e6-bcecbdde09c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4edddc33-e239-4fb0-9a8c-63f2293a81ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8886dad0-211d-4f0b-a2df-2874cb9f78f1",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8886dad0-211d-4f0b-a2df-2874cb9f78f1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4edddc33-e239-4fb0-9a8c-63f2293a81ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "29e3e5f0-97eb-426b-902b-0a6b601f2fff",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to the last two pages of their Explore-Style App Project Guide, where the project rubric is located. Have students review each category carefully and mark where they believe their project currently falls. Encourage honest self-assessment — the goal is to recognize strengths and identify areas for growth as intentional developers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are thoughtfully evaluating each rubric row rather than rushing through it. Encourage them to pay close attention to:\n- API Integration & Async Logic — Does their fetch flow work reliably and clearly?\n- Dynamic Rendering & Interaction — Does the reveal interaction behave predictably?\n- Defensive Design & Reliability — Do loading, error, and empty states work consistently?\n- Documentation & Code Quality — Is their README clear? Are their comments meaningful?\n\nPrompt students to support their self-ratings with specific evidence from their app.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that self-assessment is a professional skill. Developers regularly evaluate their own work before release to ensure it meets quality standards. Encourage them to think beyond “Does it work?” and instead ask, “Is it clear, reliable, and well-documented?”"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "29e3e5f0-97eb-426b-902b-0a6b601f2fff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4edddc33-e239-4fb0-9a8c-63f2293a81ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "60781175-1821-4f23-8645-c91bab9a7fa9",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "60781175-1821-4f23-8645-c91bab9a7fa9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4edddc33-e239-4fb0-9a8c-63f2293a81ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5fed48df-b379-43fb-83d4-e1e10f108bd8",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 4 to complete their Explore-Style App Projects. Explain that this is their final dedicated work time before submission and presentation. Today’s focus is prioritizing improvements based on their self-assessment and peer feedback. Emphasize that they should make intentional, high-impact refinements rather than trying to change everything at once. Remind students to submit their projects at the end of the allotted time.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that professional developers prioritize clarity and reliability before release. Encourage them to focus on improvements that most impact the user experience. Iteration means making smart, focused decisions — not fixing everything. If time allows, have students do a quick pair preview and give one final piece of constructive or celebratory feedback before submission."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5fed48df-b379-43fb-83d4-e1e10f108bd8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4edddc33-e239-4fb0-9a8c-63f2293a81ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e13452e2-4c7f-445c-8e30-ef7ba469cb8d",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e13452e2-4c7f-445c-8e30-ef7ba469cb8d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4edddc33-e239-4fb0-9a8c-63f2293a81ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4a470d6a-3a48-4e95-8c36-bc40918f4fdd",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Distribute several sticky notes to each student for the gallery walk.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students begin the Gallery Walk by displaying their Explore-Style App Apps on their computers, then moving around the room to explore classmates' projects. As they visit each one, students should use their sticky notes to leave feedback, kudos, or questions following the TAG approach (Tell, Ask, Give). Encourage them to engage with a range of projects—not just those of close friends.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Frame the Gallery Walk as a celebration of learning and creativity, not a critique. Say something like: \"This is your chance to see how everyone's ideas came to life in different ways. You might even get inspired by someone else's approach!\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4a470d6a-3a48-4e95-8c36-bc40918f4fdd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4edddc33-e239-4fb0-9a8c-63f2293a81ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b9ab6e60-42ce-4cff-a4b3-707eb4081dbf",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b9ab6e60-42ce-4cff-a4b3-707eb4081dbf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4edddc33-e239-4fb0-9a8c-63f2293a81ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "650702de-3398-42c8-adc3-98e11f58b066",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ve moved from connecting to an API and rendering live data to building interactive features, strengthening reliability, and documenting your work professionally. This final reflection is your chance to capture how your app evolved — how your interaction improved, how your defensive design strengthened user trust, and how your documentation demonstrates ownership. It’s also a chance to reflect on how you used AI — when it helped you think through logic, debug issues, or refine your code.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "650702de-3398-42c8-adc3-98e11f58b066",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4edddc33-e239-4fb0-9a8c-63f2293a81ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f01b83f3-787c-48b1-8220-1ec51b1641c3",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Final Reflection and Final Self-Check sections in their Explore-Style App Project Guide. Explain that this step helps them consolidate what they learned about:\n- Integrating and managing live API data\n- Designing clear and predictable interactions\n- Implementing loading, error, and empty states\n- Writing meaningful documentation and comments\n- Using AI intentionally and evaluating its suggestions\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write, encourage them to reference concrete moments from their project, such as:\n- A bug they discovered while testing an edge case\n- A change they made to improve clarity or reliability\n- How they improved their loading or error messages\n- How they revised or clarified their async logic\n- How they evaluated, modified, or rejected an AI suggestion\n\nProbe with questions like:\n- What changed in your app between Milestone 1 and Milestone 4?\n- How did your approach to debugging evolve?\n- What decision most improved user trust?\n- What part of your app best shows ownership?\n\nIf students finish early, invite them to help a peer reflect or revisit the rubric one more time.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "- The Project Planning Guide, Milestone evidence, and Final Reflection can be used as assessment for the Process, Iteration, and AI Use rubric row, as they document student growth, debugging decisions, and evaluation of AI suggestions.\n- The Final Reflection also provides evidence for Defensive Design & Reliability, since students explain how their loading, error, and empty states strengthen clarity and user trust.\n- Code excerpts and screenshots from Milestones 1–3 can serve as evidence for API Integration & Async Logic, Dynamic Rendering & Interaction, and Documentation & Code Quality, depending on what students choose to annotate and highlight in their submission."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f01b83f3-787c-48b1-8220-1ec51b1641c3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4edddc33-e239-4fb0-9a8c-63f2293a81ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b76a0382-5fcb-4a0f-96cc-c36469697b4e",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
